---
// Pearl › 08 — Close: centred label and headline (second half muted), two paragraphs in the
// reading column, then the results grid: total screens on top, web and mobile below.
// Figma's stray closing quote after the last paragraph is dropped.
import Section from './Section.astro';

const split = [
  { value: '500+', label: 'Web screens' },
  { value: '420+', label: 'Mobile screens' },
];
---

<Section tone="white" width="wide" labelledby="close-title" last>
  <div class="column">
    <div class="heading">
      <p class="text-label-m label">Close</p>
      <h2 id="close-title">What Pearl <span class="muted">made possible</span></h2>
    </div>

    <div class="body text-body-xl">
      <p>
        Later in the project, the client asked for two complex screens, a product details page for
        a plan and the plan page itself, before a meeting that started in 20 minutes. The
        wireframes were already approved, so only the UI was left. I had both ready in 10 minutes,
        built entirely by dragging in components and swapping in the content. The client was
        really happy, both with how quickly they came back and with how they looked.
      </p>
      <p class="text-body-l-mobile">
        That speed held at scale too. One Friday, every mobile shop journey was due at once, 15
        journeys and more than 70 screens, and after a long day, every one of them went out. None
        of it depended on luck. The same four files, token layers and base components carried
        through from the first screen to the last. The only screens that ever needed reworking were
        the two built before Pearl existed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <dl class="results">
    <div class="result total">
      <dt class="text-label-m">Total screens delivered</dt>
      <dd class="text-number-m">920+</dd>
    </div>
    <div class="split">
      {split.map(({ value, label }) => (
        <div class="result">
          <dt class="text-label-m">{label}</dt>
          <dd class="text-number-m">{value}</dd>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </dl>
</Section>

<style>
  /* Narrow reading column (612) centred in the wide band. */
  .column {
    max-width: 612px;
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  .heading {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-24);
    text-align: center;
  }

  .label {
    color: var(--text-tertiary);
  }

  /* Balanced so it splits "What Pearl / made possible", as in Figma. */
  h2 {
    color: var(--text-primary);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .muted {
    color: var(--text-light);
  }

  /* Figma gap 18, snapped to 16. */
  .body {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-16);
    margin-top: var(--space-40);
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  .results {
    margin: var(--space-56) 0 0;
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    background-color: var(--pearl-bg);
  }

  /* Label sits under the number visually; dt comes first for screen readers. */
  .result {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column-reverse;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-12);
    text-align: center;
  }

  .total {
    padding-block: var(--space-40);
  }

  /* Figma gap 20, snapped to 24. */
  .split {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: var(--space-24);
  }

  .split .result {
    padding: var(--space-40) var(--space-32);
  }

  dt {
    color: var(--text-tertiary);
  }

  dd {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text-pearl-tertiary);
  }

  /* Mobile: label and headline left-aligned (user); the stats stay centred. */
  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .body {
      margin-top: var(--space-24);
    }

    .heading {
      align-items: flex-start;
      text-align: left;
    }

    .split {
      gap: 0;
    }

    .split .result {
      padding-inline: var(--space-16);
    }
  }
</style>
